schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Once included in a block, a transaction becomes an immutable part of blockchain history
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "Once the transaction is included in a block, it becomes an immutable
    part of the blockchain's history."
  evidence: >-
    Direct quotation attributed to "[Source S]" in how-transactions-work.md's
    "Confirmation" step, which the article's References section identifies
    as Ledger Academy: How Does a Blockchain Transaction Work.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/how-transactions-work.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used to explain why the number of confirmations (blocks added on top)
    matters — the deeper a transaction is buried, the more computationally
    infeasible it is to reverse.
  uncertainty: >-
    Quote reproduced as given in the article; "immutable" is a practical, not
    absolute, property (contingent on the chain's ongoing security/consensus
    assumptions) — not independently re-verified for this ingestion.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/how-transactions-work.md
    transformation: quoted
    authorship: ai-assisted
